# Page / Full-Page Loaders

Use animated spinners for **larger loading surfaces** (root/auth route fallbacks, suspense boundaries, standalone waits). Prefer **skeletons** for panel feature routes (see `.cursor/rules/loaders-skeletons.mdc`).

## Components

| Component | Path | Use |
|-----------|------|-----|
| `PageSpinner` | `@/components/loaders/page-spinner` | Centered animated spinner (`react-loader-spinner` Oval) |
| `FullPageLoader` | `@/components/loaders/full-page-loader` | Full viewport wrapper around `PageSpinner` |
| `RouteLoading` | `@/components/loaders/route-loading` | `loading.tsx` helper (`full-page` / `panel`) |
| `PanelRouteLoading` | `@/components/loaders/panel-route-loading` | Legacy panel content spinner — **avoid for new feature routes** |

## When

- `FullPageLoader` / `PageSpinner`: root `loading.tsx`, auth `loading.tsx`, custom suspense fallbacks outside panel chrome.
- Panel **feature** `loading.tsx`: use `Panel*PageSkeleton`, not `PanelRouteLoading`.
- Panel **root** `loading.tsx`: `RouteLoading variant="panel"` is fine.

## Pattern

```tsx
import { PageSpinner } from "@/components/loaders/page-spinner";

<Suspense fallback={<PageSpinner label="Loading data…" />}>
  <AsyncSection />
</Suspense>
```

## Do not

- Import `react-loader-spinner` directly in feature code — go through `PageSpinner`.
- Use `FullPageLoader` inside panel content (use a skeleton or `RouteLoading variant="panel"`).
- Reach for `PanelRouteLoading` on new feature pages when a skeleton template fits.
